w that the immune  system is is protective it does protect  us against infection but that  occasionally  when it's  activated in a heightened way  or an aberrant way it can lead to immune  pathologies that are detrimental  and so the next question we had was  which of these products that are  elicited by dengue from mast cells  actually are causing this vascular  leakage and hemorrhaging  in particular  we focused in immediately on something  that is mast cell specific we wanted to  look at the mast cell proteases because  we thought  you know while many cytokines and lipid  mediators can be made in the context of  infection by numerous cell types if  we're looking at something mass cell  dependent we really wanted to view it  from the perspective of understanding  which mast cell specific products could  be contributing  so two of the products that we focused  on are proteases um tryptase for example  and chymase  chymase is an angiotensin-converting  enzyme tryptase is uh known to cleave  par receptors between endothelial cells  and both of these have uh you know other  functions in vivo  that are pro-inflammatory so to begin to  understand which of these could be  inducing the endothelial permeability  that we observe during dengue we started  by culturing primary endothelial cells  on  as a monolayer and you can see we imaged  them here we stained them for the  tubulin which allows us to see their  structure uh the dappy for the nucleus  and also uh zo1 which is a tight  junction protein and that you can see  just ship is found at the border of the  cells it's important for holding these  cells together  and when we treat the cells that we've  created as a monolayer with either  tryptase or kymase we observe that both  of them were able to induce some degree  of permeability of these monolayers so  for example  on the left-hand side at high  concentration you can see that tryptase  has  really completely  eliminated this zo1 staining at the  border of cells and 