ngue  specific  envelope specific antibody  we can see that all these little tiny  granules have been released in the  surrounding tissue  so i'm going to for the sake of time  just summarize some of our early  findings on how mast cells respond to  dengue  and that is that we saw that they're  very highly protective in the skin  during cutaneous dengue infection so  when the mosquito injects muscles are  activated they release these granules  these have a pro-inflammatory effect in  the skin at the local site and that  promotes viral clearance at the early  stages  particularly we get recruitment of these  cells in the corner in k cells and t  cells  and in kt cells  which  clear the infection and  prevent the virus from spreading to the  draining lymph nodes to the same extent  so we get much lower levels of early  viral infection in animals that  have mast cells compared to mast cell  deficient animals  and many of the mediators that are  involved in the recruitment of these  cells we highlighted here on the side  and these are also de novo synthesized  in addition to the granules that mast  cells have with proteases inside  so um but today i want to talk about the  pathology that's associated with dengue  hemorrhagic fever and uh when i first  started this project now over more than  10 years ago  we were questioning uh you know this  unique spectrum of disease you see with  dengue actually has some similarities to  other  pathological contacts particularly  allergic diseases and anaphylaxis so you  can see here on the left in panels a and  b how this individual who has dengue um  is experiencing a pretty severe rash  on their skin and in the panel below  that  hello  this patient has a bleeding into the  eyes so this is a microvascular  hemorrhaging here in the eyes and these  sorts of things happen as well during  very severe allergic reactions  and in those conditions  mast cells we know are very essential  for disease as i mentioned  in part because they release all of  these mediators  including things such as histamine tnf  the proteases i talked about  leukotrienes which are also important  for vascular effects heparin  again an anticoagulant and others and we  know that together these mediators can  break tight junctions between  endothelial cells they can reduce  clotting  so based on that we wanted to first  question whether mast cells are  contributing to vascular leakage during  dengue infection and for this we use  mice that are deficient in mast cells  they have a genetic deficiency so they  don't have  any mast cells  and in this case we saw that  when we measure vascular leakage here in  this particular panel on the top right  we're measuring vascular leakage by  hematocrit which is  the concentration of the blood that is  red blood cells so you can imagine that  as you lose  the fluid in the blood vessels  as the plasma seeps out through  blood vessels that are more permeable  you end up with a higher concentration  of red blood cells  that are remaining in the blood and so  this hemo concentration is observed in  wild-type mice  that are dengue infected but we do not  see this in mast cell deficient mice and  this strongly points to mast cells as  being  an essential contributor to vascular  leakage in this animal model  and down below  we also wanted to look in humans and try  to determine whether mast cells are  activated in humans  for this we had a group of patients in  singapore and very uniquely  these patients  could be classified into having dengue  fever or mild disease or dingy  hemorrhagic fever which is really severe  disease and what initially we wanted to  just uh use a biomarker of mast cell  activation and this  we particularly used this protease which  is called chimase  this is a  really mast cell specific molecule that  is made by mast cells and stored in  their granules and not made by other  cells  and we thought it can be a biomarker of  mast cell activation and we'll  understand then if humans are  experiencing mast cell activation during  dengue infection and so indeed you can  see both patients with dhf and  dengue fever as well have heightened  levels of mast cell activation but one  important um observation was that this  seemed to correlate with severity so  that those patients with dengue  hemorrhagic fever had much higher levels  of climate in their serum  than patients with a milder dengue fever  and this really suggests that muscle  activation itself is correlated with the  severity of disease  so one other thing i told you at the  beginning is associated with severe  disease  is previous infection and  there are many different ways that it is  thought that antibodies could possibly  contribute to severe disease when you  have a secondary heterologous infection  so this cartoon shows this you can see  uh during a primary infection uh  infection by one serotype will generate  an immune memory and part of that memory  response includes the the production of  antibodies that are specific against the  first serotype  and  again on the right hand side at the top  where it says secondary homologous  infection what this diagram is showing  is that during a secondary infection  with the same stereotype  we are able to see neutralization and  blocking of infection by these  antibodies so they're very effective in  preventing re-infection  but  during a secondary heterologous  infection it's a very different story  first we can see a phenomenon that's  called antibody dependent enhancement of  infection and in this uh scenario we  know that that when you have infection  with the second serotype you can get  binding of these antibodies to that  serotype but they may not be protective  and in fact they can cause a process  called optimization  which is when these immune complexes of  antibody and virus are taken up  by  cells that express the fc receptors such  as dendritic cells monocytes or  macrophages  and this can actually lead to enhanced  infection of those cells  leading to you know worse and severity  of disease these antibodies can also uh  over here on the far right side you can  see  decorate uh infected cells and lead to  uh in case cell-mediated killing of  those cells uh which could in induce a  great deal of cytokine release and  inflam inflammation associated with the  death of these infected cells but what  i'm going to talk to you about at length  today is this process is depicted right  in the middle at the bottom of this  slide which is mast cell mediated  activation by immune complexes and we're  going to talk about how we should that  this occurs in detail  so we know that mast cell activation is  enhanced by ige but an important point  is that it's also enhanced by igg  and so you can see in the top graph  where  we have  used increasing concentrations of this  antibody 4g2 which again i mentioned is  a cross-reactive antibody that binds to  all of the different serotypes of dengue  you can see that  in the presence of increasing  concentrations of antibody we have  increasing mast cell degranulation and  that's shown here at the bottom as well  where in the control cells the cranials  are very nice and tightly packed inside  the mast cells  but with dengue activation we begin to  see release of these granules onto the  slide around this mast cell that's been  activated by the virus and then on the  far right you can appreciate that this  is much more extreme in the presence of  a enhancing antibody  well it's not  oops  it's not only  the  it's not only the muscle degranulation  that's enhanced it's also  the  vascular leakage so when we use this in  vivo model sash mites here are to  indicate the mast cell deficient mice  and we compared them to wild-type mice  um and we measured their vascular  leakage after dengue infection so on the  left we use hematocrit which i spoke  about previously and on the right we  used evan's blue dye which is a  injectable dye that you can then detect  as it leaks into tissues and again in  the wild type which is shown here in  magenta we can see that there is a  significant increase in with dengue  alone uh when we give it with just an  isotype control antibody that's not  specific i mean and that we saw  previously as well but in the presence  of uh antibody we get greatly enhanced  vascular leakage  and again this is shown by both of the  methods in contrast in the sash mice  which lack mast cells not only do we  lose the  direct  vascular leakage that's mast cell  dependent so that the infection alone  induces uh  vascular leakage that's really close to  the baseline  but again we lose the antibody enhanced  component of that as well and again  that's shown  with the turquoise color  in both of these two scenarios  so  um an important point is that this is  really happening through the mast cells  receptor for igg so you can see on the  left hand side we treated mast cells  with  a  this 4g ii antibody that crossed reacts  between different dengue serotypes and  we challenged them with the muscles then  with dengue and you can see that both  dengue alone and the uv inactivated  dengue induced a significant increase in  degranulation above the baseline level  caused by dengue alone  and then you know the overall baseline  is shown by this gray line at the middle  but when we use  mast cells particularly bone marrow  drive mast cells that lack  the fc gamma r3 receptor which is the  activating sc receptor on mast cells for  detecting igg immune complexes we can  see that this antibody dependent  enhancement is lost  so  again we can uh test this in vivo so  we made mast cells from either wild-type  mice or  fc gamma r3 knockout mice  and after generating those mast cells we  injected them into mast cell deficient  mice and this allows engraftment of  those mast cells and in that context we  can then see the function of the mast  cells in vivo and animals themselves  after infection  we also then sensitize these animals  with an antibody against dengue and  followed by a dengue challenge and so  you can see here in those animals that  are reconstituted  with the esee gamma r3 knockout  bone marrow drive mast cells we see  far reduced vascular leakage again in  terms of the hematocrit  readings as well as the dye leakage into  the tissues so this really suggests that  mast cells can be activated by immune  complexes that antibodies  are able to enhance the mast cell's  effect on the vasculature  during dengue infections  and then we also went back to our dengue  cohort  and we looked at those patients that had  dhf and we split those patients into  those that were having a primary dengue  infection and those that were having a  secondary dengue infection and this was  determined serologically in advance  and we saw that when we  compare the levels of climate uh which  are analogous to the levels of mast cell  activation  it's much heightened in the context of  secondary dengue infection compared to a  primary dengue infection so even for  those severe patients  the ones that are having a secondary  infection are having much higher levels  of mast cell activation  so uh just to summarize this part so far  we can see that mast cells mediate  enhanced pathology during secondary  dengue infection the dengue specific igg  can augment the release of mast cell  products this seems to happen through  activating fc receptors on the surface  of mast cells and we think this is  another mechanism for how  mast cell how antibodies can exacerbate  immune pathology during secondary dengue  infection and one that is mast cell  dependent so i still want to emphasize  that early in infection we do know that  mast cells are  highly protective when the infection is  localized and it's early in the disease  time course  but we also see that their products can  contribute to severe disease and i think  this is very similar to many other  immunological contexts that we think  about where we know that the immune  system is is protective it does protect  us against infection but that  occasionally  when it's  activated in a heightened way  or an aberrant way it can lead to immune  pathologies that are detrimental  and so the next question we had was  which of these products that are  elicited by dengue from mast cells  actually are causing this vascular  leakage and hemorrhaging  in particular  we focused in immediately on something  that is mast cell specific we wanted to  look at the mast cell proteases because  we thought  you know while many cytokines and lipid  mediators can be made in the context of  infection by numerous cell types if  we're looking at something mass cell  dependent we really wanted to view it  from the perspective of understanding  which mast cell specific products could  be contributing  so two of the products that we focused  on are proteases um tryptase for example  and chymase  chymase is an angiotensin-converting  enzyme tryptase is uh known to cleave  par receptors between endothelial cells  and both of these have uh you know other  functions in vivo  that are pro-inflammatory so to begin to  understand which of these could be  inducing the endothelial permeability  that we observe during dengue we started  by culturing primary endothelial cells  on  as a monolayer and you can see we imaged  them here we stained them for the  tubulin which allows us to see their  structure uh the dappy for the nucleus  and also uh zo1 which is a tight  junction protein and that you can see  just ship is found at the border of the  cells it's important for holding these  cells together  and when we treat the cells that we've  created as a monolayer with either  tryptase or kymase we observe that both  of them were able to induce some degree  of permeability of these monolayers so  for example  on the left-hand side at high  concentration you can see that tryptase  has  really completely  eliminated this zo1 staining at the  border of cells and we can even see  cells peeling up off of  of the  [Music]  tissue culture plate um in the lower  concentration we start seeing some  punctate staining  between cells that really is indicative  that these  tight junctions are beginning to be  broken down and that's similar to what  we see at high concentrations of  climates  we also were curious if this happens in  vivo so to begin with uh we  produced uh tryptase and kymase uh as  recombinant proteins to inject into  recipient animals in the absence of  dengue infection and what we observed is  that  given the same concentration  of tryptacerchimaes  there was a significant increase in the  hematocrit  or vascular leakage in vivo that was  entirely dependent on the protein itself  importantly uh  this tryptace and khymis were given  concentrations very similar to what's  observed in dhf patients and we saw that  at the same dose uh tryptase was much  more effective inducing vascular leakage  compared to climates and and this did  not occur with an innocuous control  protein which we used here which is  ovalbumin  similarly  we also borrowed from some of the  allergy and anaphylaxis literature where  a drop in temperature is a key marker of  um  of shock and can be used to read out the  the presence of shock after anaphylaxis  and so we wanted to use that system here  when we injected tryptace and kymase we  noted that there was a very significant  drop in temperature that's indicative of  shock that  occurred at the same level of tryptase  that is observed in dhf patients so this  really showed us that  both tryptace and khymit can induce  vascular leakage but tryptase is much  more strongly associated with shock  and this also occurred in humans so we  used some serum samples to test for for  tryptase and uh these patients were  graded according to the severity of the  dhs that they experienced so grade two  dhf is associated with vascular leakage  and hemorrhaging but not necessarily  with shock  grade 3 dhf is associated with shock but  grade 4 dhf is very severe it involves  shock  in the absence of a pulse and is  is indicative of um  circulatory failure  in contrast to that uh fatal  fatal dhf is really very similar to  grade four and and there's not a  distinguishing feature other than the  survival so you can see here that  there's a very strong correlation  between the levels of serum tryptase and  the grade of shock that the patients  were experiencing  so then the next question is of course  if mast cells are contributing to dengue  immune pathology are we able to target  mast cells to limit  the disease and this is an exciting  possibility because we know that there  are many many drugs that are on the  market already that target either mast  cells or their products and they've been  used  in the clinic for decades and they're  very safe so some of these include mast  cell stabilizers uh such as chromoline  and ketotin  leukotriene receptor antagonists and tnf  blockers and so we wanted to test  different types of drugs and identify  those that might be effective against  dengue  this is one of our first studies we did  you can see that  in animal models that were infected with  dengue we treated this is a mouse model  with either chrome one ketotiphan or  tnf we also used separately that's not  shown here the leukotriene receptor  antagonist and we saw that only  the chromoline and ketotophen were very  highly effective in blocking vascular  leakage by both of our different methods  in terms of evidence  dye leakage into the tissues as well as  the hematocrit  we also know that uh in animals that are  infected with dengue were able to detect  very striking changes that occur with  respect to the transcriptional processes  happening in the spleen shown here as an  example but also the liver and other  target organs and many of these pathways  that are induced such as the interferon  response pathway cytokine responses and  lipid metabolism we saw were reduced or  reversed  in in the presence of ketotin  as a mast cell stabilizing drug  we also used a tryptace inhibitor we  used a drug called nafamostat mesolithic  and in this case uh we gave the animals  in the thermostat methylate and tested  the enzymatic activity of tryptase we  saw in panel b here that it was reduced  and  and when they were treated we could  again measure the vascular leakage and  you can see in panel a a very  significant drop in vascular leakage in  the presence of nephamosthat methylate  and this really affected the vascular  leakage specifically for example another  sign of severe dengue is a  thrombocytopenia or a drop in platelets  and you can see that was not affected so  this effect on tryptace was very  specifically associated with the uh  preventing vascular leakage and in  penalty you can see that this  inhibition occurred without greatly  affecting  the viral titers itself  and what this looks like in vivo we can  measure  with a video the  vascular leakage over time so over time  you can see in the control in the very  top panel we just see the blood uh  flowing through the blood vessels but we  don't see vascular leakage at the steady  state  in the presence of dengue however over  time after dye injection you can see  that these images captured at different  times show the gradual leakage of the  dye into the tissue itself but this is  completely prevented in the context of  nephamostat mesolate treatment and  that's just shown here quantitatively  where over time you can see the green  line shows an increase in the leakage of  the dye whereas the blue line shows that  this uh the famous that methylate is  able to completely protect against  vascular leakage  so some key points i wanted to make to  to  summarize are that massive proteases are  really key for dengue-induced vascular  leakage  we know that the mast cell activation is  a strong correlate of dengue severity um  one point i'd like to make is that most  of these serum samples that we collected  and that i showed you um that data was  collected at very early time points so  before the patients actually develop  severe disease and so we think kaimase  is a really strong candidate as an early  biomarker of disease severity and that  perhaps some of the processes that are  being initiated by inflammation early on  are are climace dependent and then again  we see tryptase which is not as strong  of a biomarker just because of its  kinetics of breakdown quick  quick breakdown in vivo but it seems to  be very important  for  actually developing vascular leakage  and is strongly associated with shock  particularly  so um between these two proteases we  think that this uh these are possible uh  promising therapeutic targets  for preventing severe dengue and this is  something that our group is continuing  to study  so i just want to  summarize a bit what we know about  vascular leakage during dengue and the  immune pathology associated with it so  we know that there are innate processes  that can regulate vascular leakage for  example when the virus infects target  cells we know that we can have the  production of pro-inflammatory mediators  such as interferons such as cytokines  and the virus proteins themselves  including products such as  ns1 they may directly act on various  cell types in the body and cause some  aspects of the pathology that we observe  and associate with dengue we also know  that people who are having a secondary  dengue infection  can have more severe disease and i  talked about several ways that  antibodies can do this  both by enhancing infection and also  through enhancing mast cell activation  but t cells are an important component  as well perhaps the t cell response and  the memory t cell response can also lead  to enhanced activation and release of  pro-inflammatory mediators in the  context of secondary infection and some  of these could contribute as well to the  pathology but i think central to  what we have studie